{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's central claim—that no evidence within a single evaluation can distinguish genuine reliability gain from mere compliance—is self-undermined by its own listed counterconditions. It explicitly concedes that a single evaluation containing process-tracing data (e.g., panel deliberation transcripts) can 'directly evidence rule-application rather than box-ticking.' If transcript data within the same evaluation can show that panelists are causally applying the rule rather than mechanically checking boxes, then the distinction is not, in principle, unavailable within a single evaluation. The target's own countercondition falsifies its universal 'No evidence within a single evaluation can distinguish...' claim. At best the target has argued that a purely outcome-based single evaluation cannot do so, but the problem does not restrict evidence to outcomes; it asks what evidence *within a single evaluation* would distinguish the two, and process data collected during that evaluation is a direct answer. By overclaiming the in-principle impossibility while simultaneously offering a pathway to the in-principle possibility, the target fails to answer the problem as stated.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A single evaluation collects panel deliberation transcripts showing members explicitly reasoning 'the checklist says to check X before concluding Y; let me verify X on this case' and adjusting answers when X fails, while a corrupted-checklist control arm in the same evaluation shows accuracy dropping precisely on cases where the corrupted rule diverges from the valid one. This is evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine rule-application from mere compliance tracking the right answer, yet the target's universal claim denies its possibility."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}